<SiteLayout title="About ConvertAnyFile — Browser-First File Conversion" description="ConvertAnyFile is a browser-first file conversion platform focused on fast workflows, local processing, and clear format support.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>About</span></div><span class="eyebrow">About ConvertAnyFile</span><h1>File conversion without turning every file into a server upload.</h1><p>ConvertAnyFile is being built as a focused conversion platform: a shared interface, separate converter engines, and local processing wherever the workflow can be reliable in the browser.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-shell"><article class="site-container article"><h2>Why this platform exists</h2><p>File formats create small but constant friction. A phone photo is saved as HEIC, a website wants WebP, a graphics tool expects PNG, or a recipient simply needs a file they can open. Conversion should be fast enough that the tool gets out of the way.</p><p>ConvertAnyFile starts with image conversion because that category can be handled well with a browser-based WebAssembly engine. The product is intentionally not claiming support for every possible file type on day one.</p><h2>A shared platform, not a pile of copied tools</h2><p>The site uses one shared page layout, navigation system, converter shell, batch queue, download workflow, FAQ content layer, and SEO route registry. Format-specific engines plug into that platform. That means a future audio, archive, or data converter can reuse the same user experience without duplicating the entire website.</p><h2>Local first</h2><p>For supported local converters, file bytes are processed in the browser session. This reduces the need for upload queues, temporary conversion storage, server cleanup jobs, and accounts just to convert a file.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Reliability before format count</strong><p>New categories should be added only when their browser workflow is dependable enough to explain honestly, test automatically, and protect with sensible memory limits.</p></div><h2>What is live today</h2><p>The image converter supports batch processing, sequential conversion, progress, cancel, retry, per-file downloads, and ZIP downloads. Format support is checked against both file signatures and runtime codec capabilities.</p></article></section>
</SiteLayout>
